WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.409 --> 00:00:06.250
NOKIA:
This is likely the most recognizable ringtone

00:00:06.250 --> 00:00:12.019
of all time, the nostalgic anthem of the early
2000s signifying the transition into the new

00:00:12.019 --> 00:00:13.370
mobile world.

00:00:13.370 --> 00:00:18.070
It was a time when Nokia was the most dominant
cellphone manufacturer in the world.

00:00:18.070 --> 00:00:23.949
They controlled over 50% of the entire global
mobile market selling over 100 million phones

00:00:23.949 --> 00:00:25.450
every single year.

00:00:25.450 --> 00:00:30.630
This not only made them a mobile giant but
a corporate giant with a peak market cap of

00:00:30.630 --> 00:00:32.890
nearly $300 billion.

00:00:32.890 --> 00:00:37.510
But since then, it has only been bad news
for Nokia.

00:00:37.510 --> 00:00:42.190
As smartphones entered the market, Nokia’s
annual sales fell off a cliff down to less

00:00:42.190 --> 00:00:44.220
than 20 million phones per year.

00:00:44.220 --> 00:00:48.420
Their annual revenue collapsed even harder
as it crashed from $60 billion down to just

00:00:48.420 --> 00:00:49.590
$6 billion.

00:00:49.590 --> 00:00:55.050
In fact, during the worst of it, they were
losing $6 billion on an annual basis meaning

00:00:55.050 --> 00:00:58.970
that their expenses were twice their revenue.

00:00:58.970 --> 00:01:02.289
Microsoft would try coming to the rescue as
they bought out Nokia’s phone business for

00:01:02.289 --> 00:01:08.610
$7.2 billion but not long after, Microsoft
would end up writing off the entire acquisition

00:01:08.610 --> 00:01:12.330
price plus more as a complete loss.

00:01:12.330 --> 00:01:17.340
And they would turn around and sell off the
remaining parts for just $350 million.

00:01:17.340 --> 00:01:22.119
But, despite all of this, Nokia is somehow
still around.

00:01:22.119 --> 00:01:25.479
In fact, they’re still worth just over $20
billion.

00:01:25.479 --> 00:01:30.100
They’ve even reentered the phone market
after Microsoft left and they’re doing surprisingly

00:01:30.100 --> 00:01:31.170
well.

00:01:31.170 --> 00:01:36.840
Just last year, their smartphone sales tripled
as the Nokia C100 became the 15th best selling

00:01:36.840 --> 00:01:38.369
smartphone in the US.

00:01:38.369 --> 00:01:43.850
As such, they’ve been posting some of their
best quarters in last 15 years pulling in

00:01:43.850 --> 00:01:46.180
nearly $5 billion per year.

00:01:46.180 --> 00:01:50.909
Of course, this is still a far cry from their
once ubiquitous position in the market.

00:01:50.909 --> 00:01:55.780
But, for a company that most would assume
went bankrupt year ago, Nokia is actually

00:01:55.780 --> 00:01:56.890
doing quite well.

00:01:56.890 --> 00:02:01.939
In fact, you could easily argue that Nokia
is super undervalued as their PE ratio currently

00:02:01.939 --> 00:02:03.630
sits at a measly 5.

00:02:03.630 --> 00:02:08.590
So, join me as we take a look at how Nokia
has managed to not just stick around but recover

00:02:08.590 --> 00:02:13.540
in the era of smartphones.

00:02:13.540 --> 00:02:21.140
THE FALL BEGINS:
Jumping straight into the beginning of the

00:02:21.140 --> 00:02:25.019
downfall, we have Nokia’s apex year: 2007.

00:02:25.019 --> 00:02:29.930
This was the fateful year that the iPhone
was announced, so it’s not surprising that

00:02:29.930 --> 00:02:34.160
it was also the peak of Nokia but it wasn’t
all doom and gloom.

00:02:34.160 --> 00:02:39.260
In fact, Nokia actually already had a foot
in the smartphone race before the iPhone.

00:02:39.260 --> 00:02:44.879
In 2005, they released Linux based mobile
operating system called Maemo which they shipped

00:02:44.879 --> 00:02:47.240
with their Nokia 770 tablet.

00:02:47.240 --> 00:02:50.849
They were also relatively early to making
touch screen phones, the first of which they

00:02:50.849 --> 00:02:53.209
launched in October 2008.

00:02:53.209 --> 00:02:58.040
But while Nokia had the right overall idea,
they fell short from several aspects starting

00:02:58.040 --> 00:02:59.040
with software.

00:02:59.040 --> 00:03:03.269
For the longest time, Nokia was always known
for hardware.

00:03:03.269 --> 00:03:06.420
They made some of the most durable and reliable
phones on the market.

00:03:06.420 --> 00:03:12.519
In fact, people would often describe their
phones as being bricks but what Nokia didn’t

00:03:12.519 --> 00:03:17.040
realize is that people were willing to sacrifice
durability for functionality.

00:03:17.040 --> 00:03:21.890
Phones were rapidly evovling from being calling
devices to being small computers, something

00:03:21.890 --> 00:03:23.989
that Nokia completely overlooked.

00:03:23.989 --> 00:03:29.420
As such, instead of embracing Android and
coming out as the main competitor to the iPhone,

00:03:29.420 --> 00:03:33.650
Nokia wasted their time making their own crappy
OS called Symbian.

00:03:33.650 --> 00:03:38.640
Not only was Symbian clunky and lacking basic
functionality, but more importantly, it had

00:03:38.640 --> 00:03:44.040
virtually no support from developers meaning
that users were more or less limited to the

00:03:44.040 --> 00:03:45.040
in house apps.

00:03:45.040 --> 00:03:49.349
But, it wasn’t just the software side of
things where Nokia was lacking.

00:03:49.349 --> 00:03:52.489
Pretty soon, they were lacking on the hardware
side of things as well.

00:03:52.489 --> 00:03:57.959
It seems that Nokia thought that hardware
just meant durability as they would neglect

00:03:57.959 --> 00:04:00.650
upgrading the internal components and specs.

00:04:00.650 --> 00:04:01.790
Just think about this.

00:04:01.790 --> 00:04:06.319
Nokia was literally selling phones that didn’t
even have 3G capability while iPhones and

00:04:06.319 --> 00:04:09.120
Androids had already moved onto 4g.

00:04:09.120 --> 00:04:11.040
Yeah, it was really bad.

00:04:11.040 --> 00:04:15.180
Speaking of Androids and iPhones, Nokia also
dropped the ball when it came to branding

00:04:15.180 --> 00:04:16.199
their smartphones.

00:04:16.199 --> 00:04:20.680
Apple and Samsung hit this out of the park
with the iPhone and Galaxy branding.

00:04:20.680 --> 00:04:26.200
But, when it came to Nokia, there really was
no branding, I mean, just listen to these

00:04:26.200 --> 00:04:27.300
names.

00:04:27.300 --> 00:04:35.770
Nokia 5800 XpressMusic, Nokia 603, Nokia 701,
Nokia 808 PureView like what does 808 even

00:04:35.770 --> 00:04:36.770
mean?

00:04:36.770 --> 00:04:41.229
I have no idea and I think is how most consumers
felt as well.

00:04:41.229 --> 00:04:45.900
Nokia probably felt that the Nokia name itself
was enough to appeal to customers which brings

00:04:45.900 --> 00:04:49.540
us into our next pitfall: overconfidence.

00:04:49.540 --> 00:04:54.470
Nokia largely overestimated the value of their
brand, and it’s not surprising why, they

00:04:54.470 --> 00:04:56.970
were basically synonymous with cell phones.

00:04:56.970 --> 00:05:00.530
But, this was actually a massive disadvantage.

00:05:00.530 --> 00:05:06.690
They key point to note is that Nokia was synonymous
with cell phones, not smart phones.

00:05:06.690 --> 00:05:11.620
So, if anything, Nokia needed to work extra
hard to shift away from this association with

00:05:11.620 --> 00:05:16.600
brick phones, but they ended up doing the
exact opposite as they let this association

00:05:16.600 --> 00:05:19.010
drive customers to Samsung and Apple.

00:05:19.010 --> 00:05:26.130
So, to sum things up, Nokia was overconfident
while they dropped the ball on software, hardware,

00:05:26.130 --> 00:05:27.130
and branding.

00:05:27.130 --> 00:05:31.580
But, just as things were looking worse than
ever in the early 2010s, they would see a

00:05:31.580 --> 00:05:36.270
glimmer of hope from Microsoft.

00:05:36.270 --> 00:05:45.540
MICROSOFT DROPS THE BALL:
You see, it wasn’t just Nokia that had missed

00:05:45.540 --> 00:05:47.800
out on the smartphone revolution.

00:05:47.800 --> 00:05:52.650
Someone else that had missed out and was desperate
to make their way back in was Microsoft.

00:05:52.650 --> 00:05:57.890
As such, these two would decide to join forces
and take on the smartphone industry together.

00:05:57.890 --> 00:06:02.560
The first step in this partnership was letting
Stephen Elop, a Microsoft executive take the

00:06:02.560 --> 00:06:04.070
reigns on Nokia.

00:06:04.070 --> 00:06:09.450
In late 2010, Elop would join Nokia as their
first non Finnish CEO and just a couple of

00:06:09.450 --> 00:06:14.540
months later, he would ink a deal with Microsoft
to become their primary smartphone partner.

00:06:14.540 --> 00:06:20.770
Elop justified this gamble as a hail mary
that Nokia had to do to survive but really,

00:06:20.770 --> 00:06:23.530
he had just signed their own death sentence.

00:06:23.530 --> 00:06:28.500
The problem with the Microsoft partnership
was that it solved absolutely none of Nokia’s

00:06:28.500 --> 00:06:30.030
fundamental issues.

00:06:30.030 --> 00:06:31.660
Take software for example.

00:06:31.660 --> 00:06:36.099
Windows Mobile wasn’t a revolutionary OS
that was designed from the ground up for touch

00:06:36.099 --> 00:06:39.729
screen handheld devices like Android or iOS
was.

00:06:39.729 --> 00:06:44.330
Windows Mobile was really just a janky touch
screen version of Windows.

00:06:44.330 --> 00:06:49.539
As such, developer support was abysmal meaning
that Nokia made almost no progress on the

00:06:49.539 --> 00:06:50.849
software side of things.

00:06:50.849 --> 00:06:55.680
In fact, you could even argue that they made
negative progress as they would start supporting

00:06:55.680 --> 00:06:57.770
Bing and Bing Maps.

00:06:57.770 --> 00:07:02.300
As for the hardware side of things, this was
still largely up to Nokia because Microsoft

00:07:02.300 --> 00:07:07.440
was mainly just a software partner.

00:07:07.440 --> 00:07:12.070
And as such, Nokia would continue to drop
the ball when it came to hardware.

00:07:12.070 --> 00:07:19.580
The Lumia 630 for example only had 512 MB
of ram, no 4G, no flash, no selfie cam, and

00:07:19.580 --> 00:07:21.440
a 480p resolution screen.

00:07:21.440 --> 00:07:26.879
And Nokia had the audacity to launch this
in mid 2014 just before the launch of the

00:07:26.879 --> 00:07:28.360
iPhone 6.

00:07:28.360 --> 00:07:31.860
It’s really no wonder why things didn’t
work out.

00:07:31.860 --> 00:07:37.400
But despite this, their combined overconfidence
was higher than ever as Microsoft was even

00:07:37.400 --> 00:07:39.129
more overconfident than Nokia.

00:07:39.129 --> 00:07:43.690
In fact, they would even stage a fake funeral
for Apple.

00:07:43.690 --> 00:07:46.680
Yeah, that aged really well.

00:07:46.680 --> 00:07:50.840
The one thing that I would say Nokia actually
did improve upon was branding.

00:07:50.840 --> 00:07:57.180
The brand Lumia was far better than just labeling
their phones the 603 or the 808, but given

00:07:57.180 --> 00:08:00.610
that nothing else improved, neither did Nokia’s
position.

00:08:00.610 --> 00:08:05.830
Under Elop’s leadership, Nokia’s stock
price crashed 62%, their mobile phone market

00:08:05.830 --> 00:08:11.870
share haved, their smartphone market share
fell from 33% to 3%, and the company lost

00:08:11.870 --> 00:08:14.520
€4.9 billion.

00:08:14.520 --> 00:08:19.250
To make things worse, it seems that Elop was
more concerned about saving himself instead

00:08:19.250 --> 00:08:21.150
of saving the company.

00:08:21.150 --> 00:08:26.509
Instead of pivoting to Android or even just
expanding to Android, Elop would use the last

00:08:26.509 --> 00:08:30.449
leverage that Nokia had over Microsoft to
force them into an acquisition.

00:08:30.449 --> 00:08:34.200
Microsoft, who’s options where to either
give up on the phone market right then and

00:08:34.200 --> 00:08:40.220
there or spend $7.2 billion and have one last
chance would choose the latter.

00:08:40.220 --> 00:08:45.620
Honestly, this was a really smart play by
Elop for himself as he would return to Microsoft

00:08:45.620 --> 00:08:49.470
as an executive after dropping the ball completely
with Nokia.

00:08:49.470 --> 00:08:53.360
But, the same could not be said about Nokia.

00:08:53.360 --> 00:08:57.100
Just because Nokia had forced Microsoft to
take the plunge didn’t mean that Microsoft

00:08:57.100 --> 00:08:58.550
suddenly had a beter strategy.

00:08:58.550 --> 00:09:03.600
In fact, remember the Lumia 630 that they
launched right before the iPhone 6, well,

00:09:03.600 --> 00:09:05.990
that was after Microsoft took over.

00:09:05.990 --> 00:09:10.770
Yeah, I don’t think you’d be surprised
to hear that after such failures, Microsoft

00:09:10.770 --> 00:09:17.010
would just end up neglecting the phone business
for a few years before shutting it down completely.

00:09:17.010 --> 00:09:25.079
NOKIA RESURRECTS:
The silver lining about all of this was that

00:09:25.079 --> 00:09:27.510
Microsoft didn’t buyout the entirety of
Nokia.

00:09:27.510 --> 00:09:32.050
If they had, the entire brand would probably
be gone by today.

00:09:32.050 --> 00:09:36.890
Luckily for Nokia, they only bought out the
phone portion of their business but with that

00:09:36.890 --> 00:09:42.270
being said, this was their main business,
so Nokia was left in a pretty bad situation.

00:09:42.270 --> 00:09:48.420
All they had left was their network business
but looking back, this may have actually been

00:09:48.420 --> 00:09:49.760
a blessing in disguise.

00:09:49.760 --> 00:09:54.040
You see, selling off their phone business
forced them to figure out a way to survive

00:09:54.040 --> 00:09:58.390
without their hero product and that’s exactly
what they did.

00:09:58.390 --> 00:10:02.870
They poured all of their resources into revamping
their network business and fixing all their

00:10:02.870 --> 00:10:04.320
previous mistakes.

00:10:04.320 --> 00:10:07.680
For example, remember how they were previously
neglecting 4G?

00:10:07.680 --> 00:10:12.769
Well, Nokia would become the first to demonstrate
a 5G ready network in 2016.

00:10:12.769 --> 00:10:17.740
They also stopped relying so heavily on the
Nokia brand because well enterprise customers

00:10:17.740 --> 00:10:21.790
care way more about value, pricing, and results
over branding.

00:10:21.790 --> 00:10:26.709
And as for their overconfidence, this was
naturally completely gone as they were literally

00:10:26.709 --> 00:10:28.440
fighting for survival.

00:10:28.440 --> 00:10:33.590
None of these moves returned Nokia to their
former heights but it did salvage the business.

00:10:33.590 --> 00:10:39.220
By 2017, Nokia would return to the Fortune
500 as they solidified their position as the

00:10:39.220 --> 00:10:44.250
world’s 3rd largest telecommunications equipment
manufacturer, only beaten out by Huawei and

00:10:44.250 --> 00:10:45.610
Ericsson.

00:10:45.610 --> 00:10:49.639
And this recovery would be just in time for
Nokia to return to the phone business.

00:10:49.639 --> 00:10:54.160
You see, after Microsoft gave up on the phone
business, they would sell of the remaining

00:10:54.160 --> 00:10:57.200
parts of Nokia for $350 million.

00:10:57.200 --> 00:10:58.990
This consisted of two parts.

00:10:58.990 --> 00:11:03.089
The first was the brand rights to Nokia which
were sold to a couple of Nokia executives

00:11:03.089 --> 00:11:06.140
who created a new company called HMD Global.

00:11:06.140 --> 00:11:11.209
The second was the manufacturing and distribution
side of Nokia which were sold to Foxconn.

00:11:11.209 --> 00:11:16.110
Shortly after, HMD Global and Foxconn would
enter into an agreement to bring Nokia back

00:11:16.110 --> 00:11:17.160
to the phone market.

00:11:17.160 --> 00:11:21.840
But this time, Nokia didn’t make the same
mistake of partnering with Microsoft.

00:11:21.840 --> 00:11:26.060
Instead, they would partner with Google and
bring the first Nokia Android to market in

00:11:26.060 --> 00:11:27.940
early 2017.

00:11:27.940 --> 00:11:32.200
To be honest, given how late they were to
the market, it’s unlikely that Nokia will

00:11:32.200 --> 00:11:36.040
ever challenge the likes of Apple or Samsung
and return to their former glory.

00:11:36.040 --> 00:11:40.610
But, that isn’t to say that they can’t
have a smaller profitable business selling

00:11:40.610 --> 00:11:43.820
affordable smartphones which is exactly what
they’ve done.

00:11:43.820 --> 00:11:48.670
In Q3 of 2021, for example, they were able
to ship almost 3 million smartphones.

00:11:48.670 --> 00:11:53.750
So, on an annual basis, they’re selling
about 10 million smartphones which again is

00:11:53.750 --> 00:11:58.000
only a tenth of what they used to sell but
considering the disaster they went through

00:11:58.000 --> 00:12:01.649
with Microsoft, this itself is quite impressive.

00:12:01.649 --> 00:12:05.279
So going back to the question of the day:
How is Nokia even still around?

00:12:05.279 --> 00:12:09.740
Well, after getting their butt kicked by Apple,
Samsung, and Google, and losing their business

00:12:09.740 --> 00:12:14.300
to Microsoft, Nokia returned to business world
as a humbled company.

00:12:14.300 --> 00:12:18.240
Instead of trying to recapture what they once
had, they decided to focus on salvaging what

00:12:18.240 --> 00:12:21.680
they still had by focusing on networking equipment.

00:12:21.680 --> 00:12:26.480
And when Microsoft gave up on Nokia, some
of Nokia’s executives decided to revitalize

00:12:26.480 --> 00:12:31.269
the brand and become a small but successful
player in the modern smartphone world instead

00:12:31.269 --> 00:12:33.259
of not being a player at all.

00:12:33.259 --> 00:12:36.329
And that’s how Nokia managed to stick around.

00:12:36.329 --> 00:12:39.220
This story is completely different from Microsoft's
perspective.

00:12:39.220 --> 00:12:45.510
If you’d like to hear their side of it,
check out this video.

